# Workspace Rules LLM Guide

## Purpose

Use this command to validate shared and local rule documents in the current workspace. It checks metadata, section shape, navigation links, reference integrity, deterministic route wiring, and local content-size thresholds.

Use `enclosure workspace rules route --tag TAG`, `--path PATH`, or `--task TASK` before architecture-sensitive edits to print the smallest deterministic reading path for declared rule tags.

## When To Run

- After editing local rule documents.
- After syncing or editing shared rule documents.
- After adding or removing rule references.
- Before deciding which local rule documents need optimization.
- Before an agent needs to confirm which rules it read.
- When path or task context should infer tags instead of leaving tag choice to the agent.

## How To Read The Result

- A passing result means discovered rule documents satisfy the schema policy.
- Parse errors mean the document could not be inspected safely.
- Reference findings mean linked rule documents or sections do not match what was discovered.
- Graph findings mean the declared route wiring cannot reliably guide an agent.
- Structural findings mean the document shape does not match the expected rule contract.
- Local content findings mean a local rule body exceeded `workspace.rules.local.max_content_chars`.
- Route output is a read checklist plus a short receipt template the agent can echo before editing.
- Tag selection output explains whether each selected tag came from `--tag`, `--path`, or `--task`.

## Examples

- If a navigation reference fails, update the reference to match the discovered rule target.
- If a required section is missing, add the section only when it reflects real guidance.
- If frontmatter is invalid, fix metadata before changing body text.
- If local and shared guidance overlap, keep reusable guidance shared and project-specific guidance local.
- If a route is requested for an unknown tag, choose from the reported available tags or add a narrow tag to the right rule document.
- If no explicit tag is obvious, run route with `--path` for changed files or `--task` for known work categories such as `structure`, `refactoring`, or `verification`.
- Use `--task calibration` before architecture-sensitive work when the project contract still needs human-approved discovery.

## Agent Tips

- Fix parse errors before interpreting other findings.
- Keep rule documents navigable and narrow in purpose.
- Avoid copying shared policy into local rules.
- Confirm rule reading with: files read, reason, stop point, and checks to run.
- Rerun after every rule-document edit.
